-- allium: 3

-- Module: lifecycle.
-- Scope: knarr's own life around the annotations it leaves behind. Startup
-- reconciliation of marked pods, what the marker timestamp means, expiry and
-- cleanup of each owned annotation, leftovers after a crash, the gap during a
-- Recreate upgrade, the single replica with no leader election (§8, §9.19) and
-- the uninstall procedure (§5 stale state, §8, §9.12, §3 restart criterion).
-- Owner: ticket 23 for §9.12. The decided replica and upgrade behaviour is
-- built by the round-2 spec-then-build ticket 32.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "§9.12 Cleanup scope: never, when a pod or workload leaves scope, or also on graceful shutdown."
open question "§9.12 Expiry: the separate expiry for pod-deletion-cost and for safe-to-evict, and what knarr writes when one expires (§5 stale state)."
open question "§9.12 Marker freshness: what the marker timestamp means when writes are sparse, so a steady cost is told apart from hours of failed polls (§8)."
open question "§9.12 Restart: the conservative behaviour on restart when freshness is unknown, and the restart success threshold handed to §9.16."
open question "§9.12 Startup reconciliation: which pods are reconciled on startup and in what order, so annotations do not flap (§3)."
open question "§9.12 Crash leftovers: what happens to annotations left on pods after a crash or while knarr is down (§8 failure modes)."
open question "§9.12 Uninstall: the uninstall procedure and the manual recovery steps it documents (§8)."
